---
title: 100 Independent Linear Work-Precision Diagrams
author: Chris Rackauckas
---

For these tests we will solve a diagonal 100 independent linear differential
equations. This will demonstrate the efficiency of the implementation of the methods
for handling large systems, since the system is both large enough that array
handling matters, but `f` is cheap enough that it is not simply a game of
calculating `f` as few times as possible. We will be mostly looking at the
efficiency of the work-horse Dormand-Prince Order 4/5 Pairs: one from
DifferentialEquations.jl (`DP5`), one from
ODEInterface (Hairer's famous `dopri5`), and one from SUNDIALS' ARKODE suite.

Also included is `Tsit5`. While all other ODE programs have gone with the
traditional choice of using the Dormand-Prince 4/5 pair as the default,
DifferentialEquations.jl uses `Tsit5` as one of the default algorithms. It is a
relatively recent (2011) and not widely known method, but the theory and the implementation
show it's more efficient than DP5. Thus we include it just to show off how
re-designing a library from the ground up in a language for rapid code and
rapid development has its advantages.

## Setup

```julia
using OrdinaryDiffEq, OrdinaryDiffEqCore, Sundials, DiffEqDevTools, Plots, ODEInterfaceDiffEq, LSODA
using OrdinaryDiffEqAdamsBashforthMoulton, OrdinaryDiffEqExtrapolation, OrdinaryDiffEqHighOrderRK, OrdinaryDiffEqLowOrderRK, OrdinaryDiffEqVerner
using Polyester
using SciMLLogging
using Random
Random.seed!(123)
gr()
include(joinpath(@__DIR__, "..", "workprecision_table.jl"))

const LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS = [:ordinarydiffeq, :explicit_rk, :nonstiff]
const LINEAR_ODE_MULTISTEP_TAGS = [:ordinarydiffeq, :adams, :multistep, :nonstiff]
const LINEAR_ODE_EXTRAPOLATION_TAGS = [:ordinarydiffeq, :extrapolation, :nonstiff]
const LINEAR_ODEINTERFACE_RK_TAGS = [:odeinterface, :explicit_rk, :nonstiff]
const LINEAR_ODEINTERFACE_MULTISTEP_TAGS = [:odeinterface, :adams, :multistep, :nonstiff]
const LINEAR_ODEINTERFACE_EXTRAPOLATION_TAGS = [:odeinterface, :extrapolation, :nonstiff]
const LINEAR_SUNDIALS_RK_TAGS = [:sundials, :explicit_rk, :nonstiff]
const LINEAR_SUNDIALS_MULTISTEP_TAGS = [:sundials, :adams, :multistep, :nonstiff]
const LINEAR_LSODA_TAGS = [:lsoda, :multistep, :nonstiff]

# 2D Linear ODE
function f(du, u, p, t)
    @inbounds for i in eachindex(u)
        du[i] = 1.01*u[i]
    end
end
function f_analytic(u₀, p, t)
    u₀*exp(1.01*t)
end
tspan = (0.0, 10.0)
prob = ODEProblem(ODEFunction{true, SciMLBase.FullSpecialize}(f, analytic = f_analytic), rand(100, 100), tspan)

abstols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (3:13)
reltols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (0:10);

const LINEAR_ERROR_FLOOR = 16 * eps(Float64) *
    sum(abs, f_analytic(prob.u0, nothing, last(tspan))) / length(prob.u0)
```

```julia; echo=false; results="hidden"; error=false
assert_work_table_markdown_fixture()
```

Each sweep requests only the error metrics needed for its measurement with
`error_estimates` and attaches solver metadata tags to the results. The table
with each work-precision figure reports the best endpoint error, that point's
timing, right-hand-side calls, accepted steps, and a computed refinement rate
when the tolerance or fixed-step grid resolves one. An em dash means that the
rate or counter is unavailable or unresolved.

### Speed Baseline

First a baseline. These are all testing the same Dormand-Prince order 5/4
algorithm of each package. While they all use the same Runge-Kutta tableau, they exhibit
different behavior due to different choices of adaptive timestepping algorithms
and tuning. First we will test with all extra saving features turned off to
put DifferentialEquations.jl in "speed mode".

```julia
setups = [Dict(:alg=>DP5(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>dopri5(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODEINTERFACE_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>ARKODE(Sundials.Explicit(), etable = Sundials.DORMAND_PRINCE_7_4_5), :tags=>LINEAR_SUNDIALS_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>Tsit5(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)]
solnames = ["OrdinaryDiffEq"; "ODEInterface"; "Sundials ARKODE";
            "OrdinaryDiffEq Tsit5"]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(
    prob, abstols, reltols, setups; error_estimates = [:final], names = solnames, save_everystep = false, numruns = 100)
print_work_table(wp, LINEAR_ERROR_FLOOR)
plot(wp)
```

### Full Saving

```julia
setups = [Dict(:alg=>DP5(), :dense=>false, :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>dopri5(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODEINTERFACE_RK_TAGS) # dense=false by default: no nonlinear interpolation
          Dict(:alg=>ARKODE(Sundials.Explicit(), etable = Sundials.DORMAND_PRINCE_7_4_5), :dense=>false, :tags=>LINEAR_SUNDIALS_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>Tsit5(), :dense=>false, :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)]
solnames = ["OrdinaryDiffEq"; "ODEInterface"; "Sundials ARKODE";
            "OrdinaryDiffEq Tsit5"]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(prob, abstols, reltols, setups; error_estimates = [:final], names = solnames, numruns = 100)
print_work_table(wp, LINEAR_ERROR_FLOOR)
plot(wp)
```

### Continuous Output

Now we include continuous output. This has a large overhead because at every
timepoint the matrix of rates `k` has to be deep copied.

```julia
setups = [Dict(:alg=>DP5(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>dopri5(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODEINTERFACE_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>ARKODE(Sundials.Explicit(), etable = Sundials.DORMAND_PRINCE_7_4_5), :tags=>LINEAR_SUNDIALS_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>Tsit5(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)]
solnames = ["OrdinaryDiffEq"; "ODEInterface"; "Sundials ARKODE";
            "OrdinaryDiffEq Tsit5"]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(prob, abstols, reltols, setups; error_estimates = [:final], names = solnames, numruns = 100)
print_work_table(wp, LINEAR_ERROR_FLOOR)
plot(wp)
```

### Other Runge-Kutta Algorithms

Now let's test it against a smattering of other Runge-Kutta algorithms. First
we will test it with all overheads off. Let's do the Order 5 (and the 2/3 pair)
algorithms:

```julia
setups = [Dict(:alg=>DP5(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>BS3(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>BS5(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>Tsit5(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(prob, abstols, reltols, setups; error_estimates = [:final], save_everystep = false, numruns = 100)
print_work_table(wp, LINEAR_ERROR_FLOOR)
plot(wp)
```

## Higher Order

Now let's see how OrdinaryDiffEq.jl fares with some higher order algorithms:

```julia
setups = [Dict(:alg=>DP5(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>Vern6(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>TanYam7(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>Vern7(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>Vern8(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>DP8(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>Vern9(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(prob, abstols, reltols, setups; error_estimates = [:final], save_everystep = false, numruns = 100)
print_work_table(wp, LINEAR_ERROR_FLOOR)
plot(wp)
```

## Higher Order With Many Packages

Now we test OrdinaryDiffEq against the high order methods of the other packages:

```julia
setups = [Dict(:alg=>DP5(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>Vern7(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>dop853(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODEINTERFACE_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>odex(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODEINTERFACE_EXTRAPOLATION_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>lsoda(), :tags=>LINEAR_LSODA_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>ddeabm(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODEINTERFACE_MULTISTEP_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>ARKODE(Sundials.Explicit(), order = 8), :tags=>LINEAR_SUNDIALS_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>CVODE_Adams(), :tags=>LINEAR_SUNDIALS_MULTISTEP_TAGS)]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(prob, abstols, reltols, setups; error_estimates = [:final], save_everystep = false, numruns = 100)
print_work_table(wp, LINEAR_ERROR_FLOOR)
plot(wp)
```

## Interpolation Error

Now we will look at the error using an interpolation measurement instead of at
the timestepping points. ODE.jl, whose methods use lower order interpolants, is
not part of this benchmark's environment, so only the DifferentialEquations.jl
methods are compared. First the order 4/5 comparison:

```julia
setups = [Dict(:alg=>DP5(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)
          #Dict(:alg=>ode45())
          Dict(:alg=>Tsit5(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(prob, abstols, reltols, setups; error_estimate = :L2, error_estimates = [:final, :l2, :L2],
    dense_errors = true, numruns = 100)
print_work_table(wp, LINEAR_ERROR_FLOOR)
plot(wp)
```

Note that all of ODE.jl uses a 3rd order Hermite interpolation, while the
DifferentialEquations.jl algorithms use interpolations which are specialized to the
algorithm. For example, `DP5` and `Tsit5` both use "free" order 4
interpolations, which are as fast as a Hermite interpolation while
being of higher order. At higher order:

```julia
setups = [Dict(:alg=>DP5(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>Vern7(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)
          #Dict(:alg=>ode78())
          ]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(prob, abstols, reltols, setups; error_estimate = :L2, error_estimates = [:final, :l2, :L2],
    dense_errors = true, numruns = 100)
print_work_table(wp, LINEAR_ERROR_FLOOR)
plot(wp)
```

## Comparison with Fixed Timestep RK4

Let's run the first benchmark but add some fixed timestep RK4 methods to see
the difference:

```julia
abstols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (3:13)
reltols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (0:10);
dts = [1, 1/2, 1/4, 1/10, 1/20, 1/40, 1/60, 1/80, 1/100, 1/140, 1/240]
setups = [Dict(:alg=>DP5(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>dopri5(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODEINTERFACE_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>RK4(), :dts=>dts, :adaptive=>false, :auto_tags=>false,
              :tags=>[:ordinarydiffeq, :explicit_rk, :fixed_step, :order_4, :nonstiff])
          Dict(:alg=>Tsit5(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)]
solnames = ["DifferentialEquations"; "ODEInterface"; "DifferentialEquations RK4";
            "DifferentialEquations Tsit5"]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(prob, abstols, reltols, setups; error_estimates = [:final], names = solnames,
    save_everystep = false, verbose = SciMLLogging.None(), numruns = 100)
print_work_table(wp, LINEAR_ERROR_FLOOR)
plot(wp)
```

## Comparison with Non-RK methods

Now let's test Tsit5 and Vern9 against parallel extrapolation methods and an
Adams-Bashforth-Moulton:

```julia
setups = [Dict(:alg=>Tsit5(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>Vern9(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>VCABM(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_MULTISTEP_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>AitkenNeville(min_order = 1, max_order = 9, init_order = 4, threading = true), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_EXTRAPOLATION_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointDeuflhard(
              min_order = 1, max_order = 9, init_order = 4, threading = true), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_EXTRAPOLATION_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointHairerWanner(
              min_order = 2, max_order = 11, init_order = 4, threading = true), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_EXTRAPOLATION_TAGS)]
solnames = ["Tsit5", "Vern9", "VCABM", "AitkenNeville",
    "Midpoint Deuflhard", "Midpoint Hairer Wanner"]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(prob, abstols, reltols, setups; error_estimates = [:final], names = solnames,
    save_everystep = false, verbose = SciMLLogging.None(), numruns = 100)
print_work_table(wp, LINEAR_ERROR_FLOOR)
plot(wp)
```

```julia
setups = [Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointDeuflhard(
              min_order = 1, max_order = 9, init_order = 9, threading = false), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_EXTRAPOLATION_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointHairerWanner(
              min_order = 2, max_order = 11, init_order = 4, threading = false), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_EXTRAPOLATION_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointHairerWanner(
              min_order = 2, max_order = 11, init_order = 4, threading = true), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_EXTRAPOLATION_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointHairerWanner(
              min_order = 2, max_order = 11, init_order = 4,
              sequence = :romberg, threading = true), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_EXTRAPOLATION_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointHairerWanner(
              min_order = 2, max_order = 11, init_order = 4,
              sequence = :bulirsch, threading = true), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_EXTRAPOLATION_TAGS)]
solnames = ["Deuflhard", "No threads", "standard", "Romberg", "Bulirsch"]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(prob, abstols, reltols, setups; error_estimates = [:final], names = solnames,
    save_everystep = false, verbose = SciMLLogging.None(), numruns = 100)
print_work_table(wp, LINEAR_ERROR_FLOOR)
plot(wp)
```

```julia
setups = [Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointHairerWanner(
              min_order = 2, max_order = 11, init_order = 10, threading = true), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_EXTRAPOLATION_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointHairerWanner(
              min_order = 2, max_order = 11, init_order = 4, threading = true), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_EXTRAPOLATION_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointHairerWanner(
              min_order = 5, max_order = 11, init_order = 10, threading = true), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_EXTRAPOLATION_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointHairerWanner(
              min_order = 2, max_order = 15, init_order = 10, threading = true), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_EXTRAPOLATION_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointHairerWanner(
              min_order = 5, max_order = 7, init_order = 6, threading = true), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_EXTRAPOLATION_TAGS)]
solnames = ["1", "2", "3", "4", "5"]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(prob, abstols, reltols, setups; error_estimates = [:final], names = solnames,
    save_everystep = false, verbose = SciMLLogging.None(), numruns = 100)
print_work_table(wp, LINEAR_ERROR_FLOOR)
plot(wp)
```

```julia
abstols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (12:15)
reltols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (9:12)

setups = [Dict(:alg=>Tsit5(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>Vern9(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>VCABM(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_MULTISTEP_TAGS)
          #Dict(:alg=>AitkenNeville(threading = OrdinaryDiffEqCore.PolyesterThreads()))
          Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointDeuflhard(threading = OrdinaryDiffEqCore.PolyesterThreads()), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_EXTRAPOLATION_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointHairerWanner(threading = OrdinaryDiffEqCore.PolyesterThreads()), :tags=>LINEAR_ODE_EXTRAPOLATION_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>odex(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODEINTERFACE_EXTRAPOLATION_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>dop853(), :tags=>LINEAR_ODEINTERFACE_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>CVODE_Adams(), :tags=>LINEAR_SUNDIALS_MULTISTEP_TAGS)]

wp = WorkPrecisionSet(prob, abstols, reltols, setups; error_estimates = [:final],
    save_everystep = false, verbose = SciMLLogging.None(), numruns = 100)
print_work_table(wp, LINEAR_ERROR_FLOOR)
plot(wp)
```

## Conclusion

DifferentialEquations's default choice of `Tsit5` does well for quick and easy
solving at normal tolerances. However, at low tolerances the higher order
algorithms are faster. In every case, the DifferentialEquations algorithms are
far in the lead, many times an order of magnitude faster than the competitors.
`Vern7` with its included 7th order interpolation looks to be a good workhorse
for scientific computing in floating point range. These along with many other
benchmarks are why these algorithms were chosen as part of the defaults.

